WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.197 --> 00:00:02.677
Face it,

00:00:03.217 --> 00:00:05.359
we all have the same boring features.

00:00:05.778 --> 00:00:07.919
Two eyes above a nose and a mouth.

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:09.320
To an alien,

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.462
we'd probably look as similar as a flock of sheep.

00:00:13.062 --> 00:00:17.806
Yet we humans are highly skilled experts at telling each other apart.

00:00:18.466 --> 00:00:23.908
The ability to recognize faces is a superpower that we tend to not recognize.

00:00:23.989 --> 00:00:24.949
We take it for granted.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.291
We see faces everywhere,

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:28.751
but of course we make mistakes.

00:00:29.099 --> 00:00:31.821
And sometimes we see faces in trees and clouds.

00:00:32.502 --> 00:00:36.344
Face recognition is one of our brain's most sophisticated functions.

00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:37.585
But until recently,

00:00:37.905 --> 00:00:40.747
it's been as mysterious as the Mona Lisa's smile.

00:00:41.488 --> 00:00:45.771
Some people are face blind and can't recognize almost anyone at all.

00:00:46.292 --> 00:00:49.274
Trying to differentiate people is very much for me,

00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:51.816
like trying to differentiate golden retrievers.

00:00:52.015 --> 00:00:54.597
How do you tell one human being from another?

00:00:55.457 --> 00:00:57.560
Others are called super recognizers.

00:00:57.839 --> 00:01:00.281
and remember almost every face they've seen.

00:01:00.902 --> 00:01:03.824
If I was given a face of a known predator,

00:01:04.085 --> 00:01:06.548
I found him in amongst 42,000 people.

00:01:06.688 --> 00:01:07.468
I look at the eyes.

00:01:07.468 --> 00:01:08.608
Do they have thick eyebrows?

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:09.950
Do they mate in the middle?

00:01:10.631 --> 00:01:11.731
In the last decade,

00:01:12.091 --> 00:01:14.734
scientists are cracking our brain's facial code,

00:01:15.134 --> 00:01:18.617
while computers surpass us at recognizing faces.

00:01:19.718 --> 00:01:21.360
But they're also creating a new

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.084
Big Brother-style surveillance that disturbs many.

00:01:25.759 --> 00:01:30.522
It's a technology that perpetuates discrimination and harms people of color and harms black communities.

00:01:30.843 --> 00:01:32.864
They can learn to recognize when a person is happy,

00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:34.205
when a person is sad.

00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:34.825
For me,

00:01:35.066 --> 00:01:35.506
personally,

00:01:35.947 --> 00:01:36.707
this goes too far.

00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:41.330
There's a lot more that meets the eye in your face.

00:01:46.473 --> 00:01:51.957
Our remarkable face skills are already developing even one hour after birth.

00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:54.579
Newborns see extremely poorly.

00:01:55.096 --> 00:01:59.058
But they can quickly distinguish their mother's face from all others.

00:01:59.739 --> 00:02:03.481
Newborns learn to recognize the mother's face on the first day of life,

00:02:04.442 --> 00:02:07.123
sometimes just within hours of birth,

00:02:07.765 --> 00:02:10.445
and look at the mom in preference to a stranger.

00:02:11.007 --> 00:02:14.949
This is a simulation of what mom might look like to a newborn.

00:02:15.409 --> 00:02:17.350
Can see that there's an oval,

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:20.233
but the eyes and the mouths are really indistinct.

00:02:20.772 --> 00:02:23.414
So my mom initially is just a hairdo.

00:02:23.795 --> 00:02:24.656
In experiments,

00:02:24.915 --> 00:02:28.937
newborns show a remarkable attraction to face-like shapes.

00:02:29.677 --> 00:02:36.881
So if you show a baby just a little outline of a head with three blobs in it that are arranged like two eyes and a mouth,

00:02:37.521 --> 00:02:42.683
and you put that next to another outline that has those three blobs upside down,

00:02:42.984 --> 00:02:44.444
within minutes of birth,

00:02:45.125 --> 00:02:51.046
babies will look towards that face-like stimulus over and above the one that's upside down.

00:02:51.266 --> 00:02:52.768
So there seems to be this...

00:02:53.659 --> 00:02:56.562
Inborn preference for faces.

00:02:56.562 --> 00:03:04.247
University of Toronto neuroscientist Kang Li says this shows how hardwired we humans are to faces.

00:03:04.927 --> 00:03:09.591
The ability to recognize faces is actually programmed into our biology.

00:03:09.991 --> 00:03:11.752
So that's why as soon as we are born,

00:03:12.173 --> 00:03:14.735
we seek out faces in our environment.

00:03:14.935 --> 00:03:16.436
Even when we barely open our eyes,

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:18.078
we start to look for faces.

00:03:18.497 --> 00:03:20.419
And then we start to remember the faces.

00:03:20.658 --> 00:03:21.319
In fact...

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.201
There's growing evidence that even before birth,

00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:26.783
fetuses prefer face-like images.

00:03:27.204 --> 00:03:29.606
They projected this pattern,

00:03:30.086 --> 00:03:31.147
2-1-1,

00:03:32.228 --> 00:03:32.848
to the womb,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.270
on the wall of the womb.

00:03:34.570 --> 00:03:38.032
And when the pattern was a face-like pattern,

00:03:38.533 --> 00:03:41.355
they looked longer compared to the inverted pattern.

00:03:42.076 --> 00:03:42.937
Already in the womb,

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:48.262
the fetus is drawn to something that has a face-like structure.

00:03:48.701 --> 00:03:50.543
We are specialists in human faces.

00:03:59.369 --> 00:04:02.891
Our brains are so specialized at recognizing faces,

00:04:03.331 --> 00:04:05.072
we often see them where they aren't.

00:04:14.617 --> 00:04:16.538
We see faces everywhere,

00:04:16.817 --> 00:04:17.879
whether they're there or not.

00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:19.139
In fact,

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:19.840
I have a set of...

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:25.582
pictures in my lab that are everyday objects like sinks and blenders and green peppers.

00:04:26.102 --> 00:04:26.462
In fact,

00:04:26.482 --> 00:04:30.185
you can see a sink that looks sad depending on how that drain is shaped.

00:04:30.764 --> 00:04:35.846
But there's a good evolutionary reason for why we seek out faces everywhere.

00:04:36.447 --> 00:04:44.190
And this is because what's very adaptive from an evolutionary perspective is detecting that a face is in your environment.

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:47.371
Our brain has evolved to be incredibly sensitive.

00:04:47.663 --> 00:04:48.704
to the presence of a face,

00:04:48.706 --> 00:04:49.947
but of course we make mistakes.

00:04:50.307 --> 00:04:53.632
And sometimes we see faces in trees and clouds and objects.

00:04:54.333 --> 00:04:57.776
A lot of our decisions are based upon our face recognition,

00:04:58.398 --> 00:04:59.939
the decision to fight.

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:00.901
To flight,

00:05:01.841 --> 00:05:02.341
to mate,

00:05:03.762 --> 00:05:05.543
to be a friend or to be a foe,

00:05:05.964 --> 00:05:10.627
all the decisions are based upon our ability to recognize faces correctly.

00:05:10.908 --> 00:05:12.889
And it's just so essential to humans.

00:05:13.069 --> 00:05:14.591
We have to have this ability.

00:05:17.473 --> 00:05:20.636
And we don't like it when we fail to recognize a face.

00:05:21.096 --> 00:05:25.100
We've all been in that situation where you're at a party or something and...

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.341
You're introduced to someone and you say,

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:28.021
nice to meet you,

00:05:28.022 --> 00:05:28.741
and that person says,

00:05:28.762 --> 00:05:29.882
we've actually met before.

00:05:30.342 --> 00:05:32.764
And it's horrifyingly embarrassing.

00:05:35.466 --> 00:05:44.032
Our facial recognition ability starts in infancy and improves steadily over decades as connections increase between neurons.

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:50.356
The average person learns to recognize some 5,000 faces,

00:05:50.918 --> 00:05:53.819
including armies of celebrities we've never met.

00:05:54.552 --> 00:05:56.253
Do you recognize these?

00:06:01.155 --> 00:06:03.496
Are we really specialists in faces?

00:06:04.036 --> 00:06:13.819
The answer only came in 1980 in a famous experiment called the Thatcher Illusion that literally upended our view of how we saw faces.

00:06:14.561 --> 00:06:19.302
Do these two faces of former British Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher look similar?

00:06:20.123 --> 00:06:21.343
They do to most of us,

00:06:21.403 --> 00:06:22.184
say experts.

00:06:22.711 --> 00:06:23.091
However,

00:06:23.192 --> 00:06:25.492
when rotated 180 degrees,

00:06:25.812 --> 00:06:28.813
we can instantly see how different they are.

00:06:29.733 --> 00:06:34.214
So this was one of our first indications that when we look at upright human faces,

00:06:34.574 --> 00:06:36.495
we're processing them as a whole,

00:06:36.735 --> 00:06:37.735
as a gestalt.

00:06:38.175 --> 00:06:45.957
It demonstrates to us how much we are sensitive to this specific arrangement of two eyes,

00:06:46.077 --> 00:06:46.358
nose,

00:06:46.358 --> 00:06:46.858
and a mouth.

00:06:47.118 --> 00:06:51.959
I think that face recognition is so important to all of us because it drives our lives.

00:06:52.279 --> 00:06:58.022
Every interaction we have with any other human requires us to recognize that person.

00:06:58.322 --> 00:07:00.183
If we couldn't recognize that person,

00:07:00.764 --> 00:07:04.086
I rather suspect that we wouldn't have the society we have today.

00:07:14.451 --> 00:07:18.974
Yet some people are utterly incapable of recognizing any face at all.

00:07:19.814 --> 00:07:20.555
Carla Holmes...

00:07:20.795 --> 00:07:22.976
has had face blindness since birth.

00:07:23.457 --> 00:07:25.218
She can't tell faces apart,

00:07:25.699 --> 00:07:28.120
a condition called prosopagnosia.

00:07:30.101 --> 00:07:31.543
When I look at people's faces,

00:07:31.622 --> 00:07:34.545
it's kind of like looking at a golden retriever,

00:07:34.985 --> 00:07:35.564
a dog.

00:07:35.884 --> 00:07:41.088
When you see that dogs also have a nose and eyes and a mouth,

00:07:41.588 --> 00:07:46.351
but we don't expect to be able to tell easily one golden retriever from another.

00:07:46.671 --> 00:07:52.175
Trying to differentiate people is very much for me like trying to differentiate golden retrievers.

00:07:52.755 --> 00:07:55.337
How you tell one human being from another.

00:07:55.858 --> 00:07:59.440
I can never really be sure that I see who I think I see.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:01.401
It's totally destabilizing.

00:08:02.902 --> 00:08:03.743
Despite this,

00:08:04.204 --> 00:08:05.865
Carla has been reverend of St.

00:08:05.924 --> 00:08:09.067
Thomas Congregation in Montreal for a decade.

00:08:09.507 --> 00:08:11.488
It's what we do that's important.

00:08:11.848 --> 00:08:13.849
The more we become loving,

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:15.891
that's the sign of the Holy Spirit.

00:08:17.292 --> 00:08:20.213
Carla admits she still can't tell her flock apart,

00:08:20.673 --> 00:08:22.574
which can cause some members pain.

00:08:23.374 --> 00:08:24.613
Everyone wants to be known,

00:08:24.733 --> 00:08:25.975
especially by their priest.

00:08:27.555 --> 00:08:30.975
They don't want to think that they're just a faceless entity.

00:08:31.475 --> 00:08:34.917
And people don't expect that I don't recognize them and their feelings get hurt.

00:08:34.996 --> 00:08:36.216
And I understand.

00:08:36.538 --> 00:08:37.258
I get it.

00:08:37.998 --> 00:08:39.038
It can get worse.

00:08:39.677 --> 00:08:44.940
Carla recalls walking with a friend last year who greeted two young women on the street.

00:08:47.716 --> 00:08:51.178
My friend looked over at these two young women and I thought to myself,

00:08:51.618 --> 00:08:54.820
how is it that she knows two random people walking down the street?

00:08:55.901 --> 00:08:56.801
Exactly.

00:08:57.163 --> 00:08:57.642
Mom Mom,

00:08:59.384 --> 00:09:02.245
come on You do this all the time

00:09:04.007 --> 00:09:06.188
You know her You've never looked for her.

00:09:06.189 --> 00:09:08.570
Of course I have.

00:09:08.572 --> 00:09:09.991
I was looking at my daughter indeed,

00:09:10.051 --> 00:09:11.392
but I did not recognize her.

00:09:11.952 --> 00:09:13.014
I know who my daughter is,

00:09:13.093 --> 00:09:13.514
obviously,

00:09:13.973 --> 00:09:15.875
but visuals are not part of my...

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:17.696
my way of seeing my daughter.

00:09:18.517 --> 00:09:19.938
It was her voice that put me in,

00:09:19.958 --> 00:09:20.539
that it was her.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:31.009
I could see that my mom was just kind of doing that smile thing that she does when she wants people to see that she's open to remember who you are,

00:09:31.069 --> 00:09:32.310
but she doesn't quite know yet.

00:09:34.131 --> 00:09:35.613
And I had to snap her out of it.

00:09:35.653 --> 00:09:36.133
I was like,

00:09:36.313 --> 00:09:36.734
Mom,

00:09:36.834 --> 00:09:37.394
wake up,

00:09:37.534 --> 00:09:38.315
it's your daughter.

00:09:38.495 --> 00:09:39.777
I saw you this morning,

00:09:39.837 --> 00:09:40.117
you know.

00:09:40.617 --> 00:09:42.379
And when she gives me that look...

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:46.324
It's shocking because she's my mom and we have a very close relationship.

00:09:46.524 --> 00:09:48.247
Carla is not that unusual,

00:09:48.407 --> 00:09:50.709
say those who study people with this condition.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:51.629
In fact,

00:09:52.071 --> 00:09:52.211
2%

00:09:52.672 --> 00:09:54.474
of us have face blindness.

00:09:55.094 --> 00:10:00.000
That just shows that there'll be many people listening to this right now who...

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.222
have this condition but didn't realize that they had it.

00:10:03.383 --> 00:10:05.625
A couple of famous celebrities they can't recognize.

00:10:05.965 --> 00:10:07.645
They can't recognize their families,

00:10:07.706 --> 00:10:08.527
their loved ones,

00:10:09.028 --> 00:10:09.768
or themselves.

00:10:10.808 --> 00:10:13.831
Among those with the condition are naturalist Jane Goodall,

00:10:14.392 --> 00:10:15.993
British writer Stephen Fry,

00:10:16.693 --> 00:10:19.015
Apple computer genius Steve Wozniak,

00:10:19.475 --> 00:10:20.836
and actor Brad Pitt.

00:10:21.577 --> 00:10:28.644
Brilliant neurologist and author Oliver Sacks was so afflicted he had close friends wear name tags

00:10:28.927 --> 00:10:30.348
at his own dinner parties.

00:10:32.149 --> 00:10:32.809
Sometimes,

00:10:33.110 --> 00:10:35.511
Carla can't even recognize herself.

00:10:36.131 --> 00:10:38.933
If I'm standing in the bathroom and I see someone in the mirror,

00:10:38.973 --> 00:10:39.153
like,

00:10:39.154 --> 00:10:40.134
who else is it going to be,

00:10:40.274 --> 00:10:40.534
right?

00:10:40.995 --> 00:10:42.596
So I can pretty well do that.

00:10:42.956 --> 00:10:50.660
The problem with mirrors is if I'm in a department store where there's lots of different mirrors and I see myself on an angle,

00:10:50.821 --> 00:10:52.481
I don't realize that it's me.

00:10:52.682 --> 00:10:53.641
It's my reflection.

00:10:53.841 --> 00:10:56.384
I don't recognize that person as being me.

00:10:56.823 --> 00:11:04.626
I've got another patient who almost walked into a mirror in a department store because he thought someone was walking towards him,

00:11:04.786 --> 00:11:07.988
and then someone had to stop him because he was about to walk into a mirror.

00:11:08.348 --> 00:11:09.769
So it can be that severe.

00:11:12.469 --> 00:11:15.951
Carla manages by focusing on things like people's gait,

00:11:16.351 --> 00:11:16.831
voices,

00:11:17.011 --> 00:11:17.852
or birthmarks.

00:11:18.673 --> 00:11:21.394
They've got no problem with recognizing someone from their voice,

00:11:21.793 --> 00:11:22.774
from their clothing,

00:11:23.115 --> 00:11:24.474
even their walking style.

00:11:25.155 --> 00:11:25.775
So they...

00:11:26.035 --> 00:11:27.175
They can recognize people,

00:11:27.515 --> 00:11:29.057
they just can't recognize them from this.

00:11:30.256 --> 00:11:31.036
But for Carla,

00:11:31.377 --> 00:11:33.197
her inability seems normal.

00:11:34.878 --> 00:11:37.478
It's the rest of us she finds extraordinary.

00:11:38.699 --> 00:11:44.221
When I think about how people who are not face-blind look at a face,

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.881
it's as if they have some kind of a superpower.

00:11:48.423 --> 00:11:49.242
How can they,

00:11:49.482 --> 00:11:50.342
I ask myself?

00:11:51.703 --> 00:11:52.903
It's like a superpower.

00:11:53.623 --> 00:11:56.185
What is behind this mysterious condition?

00:11:56.666 --> 00:11:58.528
Scientists keep seeking clues.

00:11:59.129 --> 00:12:02.511
There's a patient who couldn't recognize fruits and vegetables,

00:12:02.692 --> 00:12:04.452
but could tell you that this was Jack Nicholson.

00:12:05.153 --> 00:12:06.194
Then you have my patient,

00:12:06.235 --> 00:12:06.654
David,

00:12:07.115 --> 00:12:09.356
who can tell you that that's an apple and a pear,

00:12:09.596 --> 00:12:10.838
but can't tell you that this is

00:12:11.399 --> 00:12:12.099
Jack Nicholson.

00:12:12.259 --> 00:12:13.280
And what that is,

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:14.841
is research gold,

00:12:15.201 --> 00:12:19.245
because it demonstrates that these two skills must be separate.

00:12:19.586 --> 00:12:21.827
That allows one patient to be good at...

00:12:22.399 --> 00:12:23.379
Fruits and vegetables,

00:12:23.519 --> 00:12:24.520
but bad at faces,

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:26.860
and the other to be the other way around.

00:12:27.361 --> 00:12:34.883
The recent discovery of how widespread this disorder is has sent neurobiologists scurrying to see how humans,

00:12:35.343 --> 00:12:36.384
even other animals,

00:12:36.544 --> 00:12:37.625
recognize each other.

00:12:38.724 --> 00:12:39.545
In experiments,

00:12:39.945 --> 00:12:43.706
faces can be identified by many animals like lions,

00:12:43.906 --> 00:12:44.447
monkeys,

00:12:44.646 --> 00:12:45.127
dogs,

00:12:45.527 --> 00:12:45.947
cats,

00:12:46.007 --> 00:12:46.467
and sheep.

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:48.581
In one Cambridge study,

00:12:49.182 --> 00:12:55.086
sheep learned to recognize the faces of over 50 other sheep and at least 10 humans,

00:12:55.486 --> 00:12:56.927
including Barack Obama.

00:12:58.389 --> 00:13:00.790
And they remembered them for two years.

00:13:02.832 --> 00:13:07.356
But even animals have trouble recognizing faces that are upside down.

00:13:10.298 --> 00:13:11.139
In another study,

00:13:11.679 --> 00:13:16.163
tropical fish were trained to remember and recognize a few faces.

00:13:16.831 --> 00:13:20.452
then spit when they saw a familiar one among strange ones.

00:13:24.733 --> 00:13:34.216
Research into face blindness has also led scientists in a new direction to those whose ability to recognize faces is utterly astonishing.

00:13:38.697 --> 00:13:41.578
Some people have extraordinary memories for faces,

00:13:42.038 --> 00:13:44.279
like private investigator Kelly Desborough.

00:13:45.164 --> 00:13:47.286
They're called super recognisers.

00:13:48.327 --> 00:13:49.047
Morgan Freeman,

00:13:49.207 --> 00:13:49.748
absolutely.

00:13:51.770 --> 00:13:52.590
Sandra Oh,

00:13:54.352 --> 00:13:55.633
clearly Jennifer Aniston.

00:13:56.353 --> 00:13:58.155
That's one of my favourites,

00:13:58.215 --> 00:13:58.976
David Attenborough.

00:14:00.057 --> 00:14:01.197
Hugh Grant all day long.

00:14:02.318 --> 00:14:05.261
Kelly can remember almost everyone she's ever met,

00:14:05.581 --> 00:14:08.724
no matter how long ago or how little she knew them.

00:14:09.184 --> 00:14:10.385
I was standing in the street,

00:14:10.805 --> 00:14:13.648
I just glanced over my shoulder for just a second.

00:14:14.144 --> 00:14:16.404
And I saw a girl standing a few people behind me,

00:14:16.664 --> 00:14:16.925
and

00:14:17.365 --> 00:14:24.350
I knew in that instant that it was my friend's sister who hadn't seen for 36 years.

00:14:24.990 --> 00:14:26.030
And it wasn't my friend,

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:27.091
it was my friend's sister.

00:14:29.192 --> 00:14:33.954
Psychologist Josh Davis was among the first to study super recognisers.

00:14:34.434 --> 00:14:37.116
He tests people regularly for this superpower.

00:14:37.956 --> 00:14:42.439
I was very surprised when I first heard about the idea of super recognisers.

00:14:42.764 --> 00:14:48.149
A super recognizer is someone who has exceptionally good face recognition abilities.

00:14:48.348 --> 00:14:52.572
They seem to have a far greater capacity in their brains,

00:14:52.612 --> 00:14:53.053
if you like,

00:14:53.293 --> 00:14:55.635
a storage of the number of faces,

00:14:55.956 --> 00:14:59.058
and also they can remember them for a lot longer.

00:15:00.019 --> 00:15:05.164
He can see through the changes in appearance that perhaps the rest of us wouldn't be able to do.

00:15:08.947 --> 00:15:10.009
In 2012,

00:15:10.349 --> 00:15:18.696
Davis was asked to help test and set up a special squad of super recognisers working for police at New Scotland Yard.

00:15:19.417 --> 00:15:22.320
They were sent to never forget a face.

00:15:23.019 --> 00:15:27.163
Officer Mike Neville started the squad and headed it for four years.

00:15:28.067 --> 00:15:34.652
The light bulb moment for me was when I saw this presentation about how certain people might be better at faces.

00:15:34.853 --> 00:15:35.473
And I just thought,

00:15:35.493 --> 00:15:35.773
yes,

00:15:35.893 --> 00:15:41.576
we've got to find these super recognisers because they'll be very useful to solving lots of crime.

00:15:43.337 --> 00:15:43.879
Eventually,

00:15:43.979 --> 00:15:50.863
Neville had about 150 police officers across London he could call on as super recognisers.

00:15:51.403 --> 00:15:57.447
And many patrolled large public events looking for known criminals or suspected terrorists.

00:15:58.752 --> 00:16:00.894
So I'm looking straight down Victoria Street.

00:16:01.194 --> 00:16:02.295
I see the male walking towards me.

00:16:02.296 --> 00:16:03.096
He's a white male,

00:16:03.156 --> 00:16:04.437
got slightly graying hair,

00:16:04.897 --> 00:16:05.978
and he's got lobed ears,

00:16:06.378 --> 00:16:07.619
very detailed inside ears,

00:16:07.639 --> 00:16:08.480
and a strong jaw.

00:16:09.641 --> 00:16:14.065
Former detective Kenny Long was part of the Super Recognizer Squad.

00:16:14.945 --> 00:16:17.247
So the area of the face that's most important to me is,

00:16:17.307 --> 00:16:17.848
first of all,

00:16:18.268 --> 00:16:19.649
and then I look at the eyes,

00:16:19.989 --> 00:16:21.270
what is the depth of their eyes?

00:16:21.651 --> 00:16:23.052
Have they got bulges below their eyes?

00:16:23.112 --> 00:16:24.974
Have they got wrinkles under their eyes?

00:16:25.214 --> 00:16:26.375
Do they have thick eyebrows?

00:16:26.715 --> 00:16:27.756
Do they meet in the middle?

00:16:28.075 --> 00:16:29.155
Is there spacing there?

00:16:30.016 --> 00:16:31.437
Then you can go down to the nose.

00:16:31.697 --> 00:16:32.897
Is there a lump on their bridge?

00:16:33.418 --> 00:16:34.658
Do their nostrils flare?

00:16:34.659 --> 00:16:36.078
Have they got a double chin?

00:16:37.159 --> 00:16:38.000
As a super recogniser,

00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:38.879
when you do look at a face,

00:16:38.899 --> 00:16:39.920
you are making measurements.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:42.221
I've never brought a tape measure with me because,

00:16:42.981 --> 00:16:43.361
you know,

00:16:43.562 --> 00:16:44.562
I think I've got one inbuilt.

00:16:45.403 --> 00:16:49.684
So you can even see people from just the smallest part of their face.

00:16:49.685 --> 00:16:53.505
You can recognise them from a part of their chin or an ear.

00:16:54.506 --> 00:16:57.207
I was watching a drama on TV and...

00:16:57.467 --> 00:17:02.431
The camera panned around and there was an actor sitting at a desk with his back to the camera.

00:17:02.771 --> 00:17:03.811
And I just said to myself,

00:17:03.912 --> 00:17:04.031
oh,

00:17:04.211 --> 00:17:05.231
that's Tim McInerney.

00:17:06.593 --> 00:17:07.073
And then I thought,

00:17:07.472 --> 00:17:08.953
how do you know that's Tim McInerney?

00:17:09.014 --> 00:17:10.214
You've only seen the back of his head.

00:17:10.535 --> 00:17:10.894
And I thought,

00:17:11.154 --> 00:17:11.996
am I going to be right?

00:17:12.216 --> 00:17:14.176
The camera pans around a bit more and the actor,

00:17:14.376 --> 00:17:15.077
Tim McInerney,

00:17:15.298 --> 00:17:15.837
it's him.

00:17:16.718 --> 00:17:20.800
I don't tend to concentrate on any specific feature of the face.

00:17:20.881 --> 00:17:22.181
I just look at the whole shape.

00:17:22.922 --> 00:17:26.824
Kelly Despera scored higher than any other super recogniser.

00:17:27.219 --> 00:17:28.679
Josh Davis has tested.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:34.241
Today she does police contract work for a private surveillance company called

00:17:34.682 --> 00:17:36.722
Super Recognizers International.

00:17:37.602 --> 00:17:43.704
She spots suspected criminals like pickpockets at crowded places like London's Wembley Stadium.

00:17:44.504 --> 00:17:49.545
I was given a face of a known predator that we were trying to find and

00:17:49.885 --> 00:17:52.346
I found him in amongst 42,000 people.

00:17:52.646 --> 00:17:53.266
It's my brain.

00:17:53.766 --> 00:17:55.367
Something inside me says yes or no.

00:17:55.711 --> 00:17:56.573
And it's as quick as that.

00:17:56.593 --> 00:17:58.514
It's just a millisecond often.

00:17:59.314 --> 00:17:59.433
So,

00:18:00.195 --> 00:18:02.676
could you be a super recogniser too?

00:18:03.617 --> 00:18:06.298
How good are you at recognising faces?

00:18:06.918 --> 00:18:09.240
Try the balaclava test Kelly did.

00:18:10.279 --> 00:18:12.562
What celebrities are behind the masks?

00:18:13.002 --> 00:18:15.543
You have only a few seconds like she did.

00:18:33.980 --> 00:18:39.721
Did you find that hard?

00:18:39.863 --> 00:18:41.222
Don't try this test.

00:18:41.762 --> 00:18:45.884
One of many Kelly did to be classified as a super recognizer.

00:18:46.365 --> 00:18:46.786
First,

00:18:47.026 --> 00:18:48.546
you see a suspect briefly.

00:18:48.986 --> 00:18:50.687
Then you must pick him out of an array.

00:18:51.227 --> 00:18:54.929
Though normally there are ten days between the tasks.

00:18:55.729 --> 00:18:56.370
Number six.

00:19:01.112 --> 00:19:01.892
Number two.

00:19:06.676 --> 00:19:08.778
Number six.

00:19:08.779 --> 00:19:10.440
14 out of 14.

00:19:10.441 --> 00:19:10.639
Well done,

00:19:10.641 --> 00:19:11.360
that's brilliant.

00:19:13.261 --> 00:19:19.307
Kelly combs through thousands of hours of CCTV footage looking for wanted criminals.

00:19:20.027 --> 00:19:23.609
The best supers can see through any disguise.

00:19:24.151 --> 00:19:27.173
as in this famous police case during the London riots.

00:19:28.396 --> 00:19:31.119
A fully masked man attacks someone,

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:35.104
but is still identified by a super recogniser.

00:19:35.745 --> 00:19:36.726
He's got a hat on,

00:19:37.086 --> 00:19:38.567
almost the top of his head is covered.

00:19:38.607 --> 00:19:42.331
But all you get as he walks away is he looks around,

00:19:42.332 --> 00:19:43.011
he turns around,

00:19:43.031 --> 00:19:44.052
and you see this look,

00:19:44.232 --> 00:19:45.273
you get his eyes here.

00:19:45.914 --> 00:19:46.254
To me,

00:19:46.596 --> 00:19:54.722
that is the iconic super recognizer identification that he was identified simply by his eyes.

00:19:55.823 --> 00:19:56.884
In recent times,

00:19:57.245 --> 00:19:59.508
this skill is especially useful.

00:20:00.488 --> 00:20:02.849
People all around the world are wearing masks now.

00:20:03.169 --> 00:20:06.812
So will this impair my ability to recognise faces?

00:20:06.813 --> 00:20:07.894
And I've found that it doesn't.

00:20:08.375 --> 00:20:13.197
Just looking at eyes can give us a great insight into whether we're making the right idea or not.

00:20:13.258 --> 00:20:15.279
You're looking at possible wrinkles in their face.

00:20:15.339 --> 00:20:16.360
You're looking at eyelids.

00:20:16.401 --> 00:20:18.001
You're looking at spacing between the eyes.

00:20:18.403 --> 00:20:20.163
You're looking at the top of their nose for the bridge.

00:20:20.483 --> 00:20:22.125
There's still many features you can see,

00:20:22.705 --> 00:20:24.047
even when they are wearing masks.

00:20:30.227 --> 00:20:31.989
Like many face-blind people,

00:20:32.528 --> 00:20:36.109
Carla always thought others struggled with faces like she did.

00:20:36.789 --> 00:20:39.850
She only discovered she was face-blind a decade ago,

00:20:40.431 --> 00:20:44.111
when she tried a simple name-that-celebrity test online.

00:20:45.452 --> 00:20:45.893
Typically,

00:20:45.932 --> 00:20:47.312
when you do a test like that,

00:20:47.732 --> 00:20:48.854
they give you faces,

00:20:48.913 --> 00:20:51.533
but they don't show you their hair or their clothes or anything.

00:20:51.974 --> 00:20:54.615
And it was kind of terrifying,

00:20:54.694 --> 00:20:55.095
actually,

00:20:55.316 --> 00:20:56.536
when I first did it.

00:20:57.320 --> 00:20:58.779
I didn't recognize the Queen.

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:01.121
I didn't recognize Elvis Presley.

00:21:01.421 --> 00:21:02.441
I didn't recognize

00:21:03.282 --> 00:21:04.082
Barack Obama.

00:21:04.642 --> 00:21:05.362
The Beatles?

00:21:05.942 --> 00:21:08.583
These are faces they're very familiar with to me,

00:21:08.843 --> 00:21:10.824
and I still did not recognize them.

00:21:12.144 --> 00:21:17.186
Out of context and without all the things that I use subconsciously for identifiers.

00:21:17.966 --> 00:21:18.346
Today,

00:21:18.566 --> 00:21:20.186
Carla wonders if this long,

00:21:20.365 --> 00:21:24.167
unrecognized problem didn't have an impact on her childhood.

00:21:24.528 --> 00:21:25.508
When I was a little girl,

00:21:25.587 --> 00:21:25.968
I was...

00:21:26.248 --> 00:21:27.009
quite a loner.

00:21:27.689 --> 00:21:29.791
I always kind of thought that people were unfriendly,

00:21:30.512 --> 00:21:32.913
that no one really wanted to be my friend.

00:21:32.954 --> 00:21:34.375
And looking back at it now,

00:21:34.495 --> 00:21:42.240
I'm just wondering whether my responses to them were not showing them that I didn't recognize who they were.

00:21:42.642 --> 00:21:42.961
I think,

00:21:43.022 --> 00:21:43.221
yeah,

00:21:43.242 --> 00:21:49.567
they might have thought that I was standoffish or arrogant or that I was making the decision not to be friendly.

00:21:51.089 --> 00:21:54.992
Actor Brad Pitt says he suffered similar problems to Carla.

00:21:56.639 --> 00:21:59.662
So many people hate me because they think I'm disrespecting them.

00:22:00.202 --> 00:22:01.123
You get this thing like,

00:22:01.322 --> 00:22:02.663
you're being egotistical,

00:22:02.743 --> 00:22:03.825
you're being conceited.

00:22:04.384 --> 00:22:05.425
But it's a mystery to me,

00:22:05.505 --> 00:22:05.745
man.

00:22:06.166 --> 00:22:07.607
I can't grasp a face.

00:22:09.628 --> 00:22:16.933
Studies comparing super recognizers with the face blind suggest they have a slightly larger fusiform gyrus,

00:22:17.334 --> 00:22:19.355
a spindle-shaped region in the brain.

00:22:20.115 --> 00:22:23.258
It shows more activity when looking at facial images.

00:22:23.658 --> 00:22:25.539
There seems to be an area in the brain called...

00:22:25.751 --> 00:22:32.296
Fusiform gyrus that a lot of studies have tended to find is great is activated more when

00:22:32.875 --> 00:22:51.906
Humans are seeing faces and it may just be the super recognizers are using areas like this in a more specialist efficient manner than the rest of us but eye tracking experiments suggest super recognizers also Instinctively see faces differently than most of us this

00:22:52.007 --> 00:22:52.807
triangle here

00:22:53.387 --> 00:22:55.669
That triangle is where all of the information is.

00:22:56.288 --> 00:22:57.328
I could put on weight,

00:22:57.868 --> 00:22:59.349
I could grow my hair,

00:22:59.910 --> 00:23:06.571
but this triangle seems to have some sort of code within it,

00:23:07.112 --> 00:23:13.732
and maybe super recognisers have got a really acute sense of that,

00:23:14.114 --> 00:23:20.454
which is why super recognisers often can recognise someone from childhood who they haven't seen for 40 years,

00:23:20.795 --> 00:23:22.935
because that configuration won't change.

00:23:23.543 --> 00:23:24.803
The legendary Tina Turner.

00:23:25.223 --> 00:23:26.384
Like the face blind,

00:23:26.684 --> 00:23:27.764
super recognisers'

00:23:27.804 --> 00:23:29.805
skills are not related to IQ,

00:23:30.385 --> 00:23:32.886
education or other memory tasks.

00:23:33.366 --> 00:23:38.567
Growing body of research has suggested that super recognition is about faces only.

00:23:39.107 --> 00:23:42.348
When you test super recognisers with object recognition,

00:23:43.489 --> 00:23:44.689
things like cars,

00:23:45.250 --> 00:23:45.929
mushrooms,

00:23:46.089 --> 00:23:46.829
chairs,

00:23:46.949 --> 00:23:47.589
flowers,

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:51.471
actually they're just average at those sorts of tasks.

00:23:51.791 --> 00:23:52.831
As a super recogniser,

00:23:52.912 --> 00:23:53.051
it's...

00:23:53.392 --> 00:23:54.552
only with faces for me.

00:23:55.032 --> 00:23:56.333
I can't recognise objects,

00:23:56.373 --> 00:23:58.673
and if the missus gives me a list to go down to the shop with,

00:23:59.193 --> 00:24:00.555
even if there's only four items,

00:24:00.914 --> 00:24:01.535
I won't remember.

00:24:01.955 --> 00:24:03.935
I'm absolutely awful at remembering names,

00:24:04.175 --> 00:24:05.997
so this kind of thing doesn't tally.

00:24:06.216 --> 00:24:07.738
It's obviously just a visual thing.

00:24:08.258 --> 00:24:10.778
I really am bad at remembering names.

00:24:11.638 --> 00:24:15.201
Being a super recogniser can be a double-edged sword.

00:24:15.921 --> 00:24:20.863
Some feel the need to hide their superpower so they won't be seen as stalkers.

00:24:21.483 --> 00:24:22.543
You'll look at someone thinking...

00:24:23.403 --> 00:24:23.524
Hmm,

00:24:23.604 --> 00:24:24.765
I actually know that person,

00:24:25.225 --> 00:24:25.926
but you don't know them.

00:24:26.086 --> 00:24:26.746
And they're looking at you,

00:24:26.846 --> 00:24:27.207
panicked,

00:24:27.267 --> 00:24:28.567
why is this guy really staring at me?

00:24:31.288 --> 00:24:32.370
As a super recogniser,

00:24:32.830 --> 00:24:34.792
the worst thing is you can't actually turn it off.

00:24:35.172 --> 00:24:35.652
Unfortunately,

00:24:35.832 --> 00:24:38.673
it's a gift and it's a pain.

00:24:39.195 --> 00:24:46.300
You imagine that you are at a theatre and you recognise someone who was at the theatre last year in the audience.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:47.000
And you go,

00:24:47.200 --> 00:24:48.941
I saw you in the audience last year,

00:24:48.961 --> 00:24:50.682
that person's going to think you're a stalker.

00:24:51.103 --> 00:24:52.584
So you can imagine that...

00:24:53.025 --> 00:24:57.374
they perhaps would want to hide their skills in those sort of circumstances.

00:24:57.414 --> 00:24:57.996
In my brain,

00:24:58.276 --> 00:24:59.800
I've got tens of thousands of faces.

00:25:00.132 --> 00:25:01.052
I wish some would go away,

00:25:01.172 --> 00:25:02.193
but they're stuck there,

00:25:02.194 --> 00:25:02.713
unfortunately.

00:25:10.857 --> 00:25:14.018
Can people be trained to become super recognisers?

00:25:14.498 --> 00:25:15.078
So far,

00:25:15.439 --> 00:25:18.540
evidence suggests the ability is mostly genetic.

00:25:19.259 --> 00:25:22.742
We don't think we can train people to become super recognisers.

00:25:23.082 --> 00:25:24.563
There's a spectrum of skills.

00:25:24.903 --> 00:25:26.003
Some people are really good,

00:25:26.563 --> 00:25:27.824
some people are really bad.

00:25:28.183 --> 00:25:30.065
Most of us appear on the middle.

00:25:30.505 --> 00:25:30.865
And

00:25:31.904 --> 00:25:34.605
I wonder whether they're a little bit like Usain Bolt,

00:25:35.125 --> 00:25:36.506
fastest man in the world,

00:25:36.546 --> 00:25:39.967
got this raw ability in him that none of us have.

00:25:42.148 --> 00:25:47.369
But limited exposure to faces can also impede people's genetic ability.

00:25:47.990 --> 00:25:48.630
As you can imagine,

00:25:48.631 --> 00:25:53.131
if you live in a tiny village in the middle of nowhere and you hardly meet anyone,

00:25:53.191 --> 00:25:56.752
your face recognition skills do not need to develop very strongly.

00:25:57.291 --> 00:26:02.874
The person who goes to a normal school with lots of children tends to be better as an adult at face recognition.

00:26:03.355 --> 00:26:05.875
So there is some experience going on there.

00:26:05.876 --> 00:26:07.955
It's not just about genetics.

00:26:11.278 --> 00:26:11.677
Today,

00:26:11.817 --> 00:26:16.559
there are eerie new challenges for us all in recognising faces,

00:26:17.059 --> 00:26:20.761
as computers generate countless artificial ones,

00:26:21.321 --> 00:26:23.782
like these and others you've seen in this film.

00:26:24.282 --> 00:26:26.243
All were taken from a site called...

00:26:26.795 --> 00:26:29.257
This person does not exist.

00:26:31.339 --> 00:26:34.662
Just how does the human brain identify faces?

00:26:35.362 --> 00:26:38.465
It's a mystery scientists have slowly been solving.

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:47.052
In a famous experiment in 2005,

00:26:47.613 --> 00:26:54.579
neuroscientist Rodrigo Kian-Kiroga attached electrodes to patients going through brain surgery.

00:26:55.135 --> 00:27:00.019
Then he showed them photos of celebrities while monitoring individual brain cells,

00:27:00.299 --> 00:27:01.301
called neurons,

00:27:01.721 --> 00:27:03.523
and made a heralded discovery.

00:27:04.544 --> 00:27:09.948
There was one patient where I found one individual neuron that I could identify firing to Jennifer Aniston,

00:27:10.369 --> 00:27:11.028
no matter what.

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.490
So I show any picture of Jennifer Aniston,

00:27:13.491 --> 00:27:14.471
the neuron will fire,

00:27:14.491 --> 00:27:15.173
will be active.

00:27:15.813 --> 00:27:17.375
If I show a picture of any other person,

00:27:17.575 --> 00:27:18.615
the neuron will be silent.

00:27:19.135 --> 00:27:22.719
And I still remember I was in front of the computer and

00:27:23.199 --> 00:27:23.860
I just jumped.

00:27:24.195 --> 00:27:26.217
I just jumped because I couldn't believe my eye.

00:27:26.397 --> 00:27:29.460
And then people start talking about the Jennifer Aniston neuron.

00:27:31.561 --> 00:27:36.605
Many scientists thought something similar might occur elsewhere in the face patch area.

00:27:37.267 --> 00:27:42.530
A popular theory was that each neuron was sensitive to one particular face.

00:27:43.271 --> 00:27:48.096
One for Jennifer Aniston and perhaps another for Oprah or your grandmother.

00:27:51.578 --> 00:27:52.899
It sounds far-fetched.

00:27:53.271 --> 00:28:03.434
But a stunning 2004 monkey study by neuroscientists Weinrich Freiwald and Doris Tsao suggests the theory isn't that far off.

00:28:04.454 --> 00:28:14.337
They placed a tiny electrode into one area of a monkey's brain and recorded the firing of single cells as the monkey watched various images.

00:28:15.258 --> 00:28:18.199
They showed the monkey over a hundred of these images,

00:28:18.679 --> 00:28:19.519
some faces,

00:28:19.779 --> 00:28:20.400
some not.

00:28:20.844 --> 00:28:23.805
While monitoring one cell in the face patch area,

00:28:24.425 --> 00:28:25.965
they got a stunning result,

00:28:27.046 --> 00:28:29.727
as we can hear each time the neuron fires,

00:28:30.107 --> 00:28:31.407
making a clicking sound.

00:28:32.787 --> 00:28:35.188
Can you hear when this cell responds?

00:28:35.588 --> 00:28:35.948
Listen.

00:28:44.231 --> 00:28:48.752
So what I hope you can hear by now is that every time there's a face shown...

00:28:49.375 --> 00:28:51.076
There's also a response of the cell.

00:28:51.657 --> 00:28:52.897
When there's no face shown,

00:28:53.856 --> 00:28:55.498
there is no firing of the cell.

00:28:59.298 --> 00:29:00.919
This cell kind of has a job.

00:29:01.119 --> 00:29:03.720
The cell's job is to signal when there's a face,

00:29:04.160 --> 00:29:04.779
and in that sense,

00:29:04.799 --> 00:29:05.681
it's a face detector.

00:29:06.500 --> 00:29:11.362
The neuron was only fooled occasionally by objects that resemble faces,

00:29:11.721 --> 00:29:12.863
such as a green pepper.

00:29:13.962 --> 00:29:14.682
A reminder,

00:29:14.962 --> 00:29:17.044
we humans see faces everywhere.

00:29:17.887 --> 00:29:18.668
It is a trade-off.

00:29:19.709 --> 00:29:23.490
You certainly don't want to miss a tiger or an enemy.

00:29:24.470 --> 00:29:31.412
So it's better to think that a pepper is a face rather than miss an enemy or a predator that you don't recognize.

00:29:32.392 --> 00:29:35.353
Their next experiment was even more remarkable.

00:29:36.113 --> 00:29:42.154
They found a second area of face neurons that only reacted to specific features,

00:29:42.775 --> 00:29:45.135
even on these simple cartoon faces.

00:29:46.039 --> 00:29:49.124
When they added or subtracted any one feature,

00:29:49.325 --> 00:29:50.886
like a nose or hair,

00:29:51.548 --> 00:29:54.873
it's easy to hear how one cell would respond.

00:29:56.315 --> 00:30:00.000
So what you can hear is a neuron.

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.441
...neuron responding to the presence of hair.

00:30:08.301 --> 00:30:13.144
Other face neurons are dedicated to identifying more facial features,

00:30:13.743 --> 00:30:15.604
like the distance between the eyes,

00:30:15.923 --> 00:30:17.204
the shape of the hairline,

00:30:17.644 --> 00:30:18.805
and the set of the mouth.

00:30:19.846 --> 00:30:23.646
Yet these are just the worker neurons of the brain's face region.

00:30:24.326 --> 00:30:28.327
Freiwald's team found another brain area where an executive cell...

00:30:28.695 --> 00:30:30.356
Put the face features together,

00:30:30.817 --> 00:30:32.057
like a jigsaw puzzle,

00:30:32.456 --> 00:30:34.738
to identify one specific person.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:42.620
If you listen closely,

00:30:43.780 --> 00:30:48.502
you will probably hear that the cell is only responding to the pictures of this one blonde boy.

00:30:55.135 --> 00:30:56.096
Not another blond boy,

00:30:56.256 --> 00:30:57.156
not another face,

00:30:57.376 --> 00:31:00.977
and it doesn't matter which way the face is presented.

00:31:03.538 --> 00:31:03.878
Again,

00:31:03.938 --> 00:31:06.799
the scientists were overwhelmed by the results.

00:31:07.179 --> 00:31:07.639
Together,

00:31:07.759 --> 00:31:14.182
the experiments revealed the underlying neurological code to how humans see faces.

00:31:14.761 --> 00:31:20.203
This was an absolutely astonishing experience.

00:31:20.223 --> 00:31:20.803
it was

00:31:21.644 --> 00:31:24.605
almost overwhelming that despite the fact that faces are very complex,

00:31:25.325 --> 00:31:28.806
the way the brain looks at them is very simple and very beautiful.

00:31:30.027 --> 00:31:35.488
The discoveries were a much celebrated breakthrough in understanding facial recognition,

00:31:36.067 --> 00:31:42.890
but they also highlighted a fast-growing question that computer scientists were already hard at work answering.

00:31:43.350 --> 00:31:46.971
If it's so simple for our brains to recognize faces,

00:31:47.612 --> 00:31:49.032
why can't computers

00:31:49.471 --> 00:31:51.413
Learn to be even better than us.

00:31:56.937 --> 00:31:57.897
For decades,

00:31:58.377 --> 00:32:04.661
scientists tried to train computers to match our human face recognition skills and failed.

00:32:05.302 --> 00:32:06.542
But in the last decade,

00:32:06.923 --> 00:32:12.166
computers got faster and mimicked our brain's neural network to make the breakthrough.

00:32:12.946 --> 00:32:14.586
What it can do today is,

00:32:14.747 --> 00:32:15.048
you know...

00:32:16.156 --> 00:32:16.336
Many,

00:32:16.396 --> 00:32:19.217
many times better than it was able to do even two or three years ago,

00:32:19.336 --> 00:32:20.557
never mind a decade ago.

00:32:21.338 --> 00:32:21.998
In essence,

00:32:22.359 --> 00:32:28.240
the computer takes a photo of your face and precisely measures its unique facial spacing,

00:32:28.740 --> 00:32:32.203
like the gap between your eyebrows or the width of your forehead.

00:32:32.903 --> 00:32:37.665
But that's exactly what we humans do all the time without knowing it.

00:32:38.644 --> 00:32:41.447
We think we identify people from their eyes,

00:32:41.787 --> 00:32:42.927
nose and mouth,

00:32:43.527 --> 00:32:44.828
but our brain knows.

00:32:45.227 --> 00:32:51.031
These change over time and unconsciously monitors deeper characteristics.

00:32:52.673 --> 00:32:53.953
Think about the shape of an eye.

00:32:54.394 --> 00:32:55.655
As I look surprised,

00:32:55.675 --> 00:32:57.036
put on eye makeup,

00:32:57.256 --> 00:32:57.576
age,

00:32:57.636 --> 00:32:58.637
develop wrinkles,

00:32:58.897 --> 00:33:00.038
its shape changes.

00:33:00.598 --> 00:33:02.920
It's not a reliable cue to who I am.

00:33:03.520 --> 00:33:04.601
Think about my mouth.

00:33:05.042 --> 00:33:06.743
As I put on lipstick,

00:33:07.063 --> 00:33:08.204
as I talk,

00:33:08.605 --> 00:33:09.645
as I laugh,

00:33:10.065 --> 00:33:12.288
the shape of my mouth changes.

00:33:12.735 --> 00:33:18.199
What never changes is how far my mouth is from the bottom of my chin or from my eyes.

00:33:18.459 --> 00:33:20.720
It comes from the bone structure of the face,

00:33:21.100 --> 00:33:23.162
so it's going to be the same when you're

00:33:23.961 --> 00:33:26.042
20 and when you're 80.

00:33:26.604 --> 00:33:27.763
When you recognize a face,

00:33:27.783 --> 00:33:32.826
you're doing zillion computations that go in parallel and that we are not conscious of.

00:33:33.186 --> 00:33:33.527
I mean,

00:33:33.567 --> 00:33:39.630
you're recognizing the distance between the eyes or the length of your nose and how big is your forehead and so on,

00:33:39.770 --> 00:33:40.770
and we're not aware of that.

00:33:41.132 --> 00:33:41.632
I don't see...

00:33:42.084 --> 00:33:43.244
The distance between your eyes,

00:33:43.884 --> 00:33:44.605
I see your face.

00:33:45.345 --> 00:33:47.926
So we are conscious of the final product,

00:33:48.406 --> 00:33:50.006
but it's unconscious processing.

00:33:50.526 --> 00:33:51.326
We don't think about,

00:33:51.427 --> 00:33:51.567
OK,

00:33:51.787 --> 00:33:55.088
now I will measure the distance between the nose and the mouth,

00:33:55.348 --> 00:33:57.188
and this will help me to recognize you later.

00:33:57.248 --> 00:33:59.369
This is something that our system is tuned,

00:33:59.689 --> 00:34:00.709
is wired to do,

00:34:00.749 --> 00:34:01.349
in a sense,

00:34:01.829 --> 00:34:03.269
and we do it automatically.

00:34:03.290 --> 00:34:04.310
We don't think about that.

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:10.132
So the ability to recognize faces is a superpower that we tend to not recognize.

00:34:10.211 --> 00:34:11.172
We take it for granted.

00:34:11.435 --> 00:34:12.616
We do it so casually,

00:34:12.736 --> 00:34:13.556
just like breathing,

00:34:13.916 --> 00:34:14.736
almost like breathing.

00:34:14.776 --> 00:34:15.717
We walk around,

00:34:15.817 --> 00:34:16.617
we see faces,

00:34:16.618 --> 00:34:17.778
we recognize who they are,

00:34:18.238 --> 00:34:19.298
just like we breathe.

00:34:20.479 --> 00:34:20.759
Now,

00:34:20.979 --> 00:34:27.102
super-fast computers are learning to do deliberately what our brain's neurons do naturally.

00:34:28.022 --> 00:34:28.662
Overnight,

00:34:28.722 --> 00:34:34.325
we're surrounded by computers that can identify human faces far better than us.

00:34:35.285 --> 00:34:39.867
Many airport security checks now use face recognition like fingerprints.

00:34:40.507 --> 00:34:40.768
Also,

00:34:41.068 --> 00:34:45.675
people routinely pay for coffee and use ATMs for selfie payments.

00:34:54.144 --> 00:34:59.271
Many people think face recognition can soon be used to help those with face blindness.

00:35:00.019 --> 00:35:05.983
Barry Sandrew is a Harvard neuroscientist who's working on an app to help people like himself.

00:35:06.784 --> 00:35:10.326
Why did I develop an application for facial recognition?

00:35:10.385 --> 00:35:10.525
Well,

00:35:11.146 --> 00:35:12.067
I have face blindness.

00:35:13.306 --> 00:35:16.768
I have trouble recognizing faces and I've always had that problem.

00:35:17.269 --> 00:35:24.833
It's intended for people who have facial blindness so that they can recognize people that they should,

00:35:25.233 --> 00:35:25.693
in fact,

00:35:25.894 --> 00:35:26.135
know.

00:35:27.554 --> 00:35:28.335
Sandrew's app.

00:35:28.695 --> 00:35:31.038
can store the photo of anyone you meet,

00:35:31.418 --> 00:35:33.298
then instantly identify them.

00:35:33.759 --> 00:35:40.563
He hopes to create a personalized face bank for Reverend Carla to help her identify her church members.

00:35:41.284 --> 00:35:48.429
And I think that we have technology today that will allow her to not only recognize the congregant,

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:52.811
but also recognize the congregant's partner,

00:35:53.932 --> 00:35:54.574
the kids.

00:35:55.614 --> 00:35:56.394
97%

00:35:56.614 --> 00:35:57.655
or better accuracy.

00:35:58.096 --> 00:36:01.898
I think that'll be a very special thing for the congregant in the congregation.

00:36:02.958 --> 00:36:04.579
But will it work to help Carla,

00:36:04.981 --> 00:36:05.721
and eventually,

00:36:06.181 --> 00:36:07.161
perhaps all of us?

00:36:07.943 --> 00:36:08.423
People like me,

00:36:08.463 --> 00:36:09.182
we're a disaster,

00:36:09.222 --> 00:36:09.344
no?

00:36:09.344 --> 00:36:09.583
I mean,

00:36:11.144 --> 00:36:12.965
I'm just meeting you perhaps tomorrow,

00:36:12.985 --> 00:36:15.027
and I won't remember the face because I'm,

00:36:15.807 --> 00:36:16.148
yeah,

00:36:16.148 --> 00:36:17.168
I'm not good myself.

00:36:17.688 --> 00:36:19.349
A big chunk of my work is on memory,

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:20.690
and my memory is horrible.

00:36:20.791 --> 00:36:24.012
So I'm afraid that I'm absolutely terrible at recognizing faces.

00:36:24.193 --> 00:36:27.635
I'm definitely in the lower half of the ability range.

00:36:28.331 --> 00:36:30.773
And the more I study super recognizers,

00:36:30.793 --> 00:36:32.112
the worse I feel I am.

00:36:35.193 --> 00:36:35.614
For now,

00:36:36.134 --> 00:36:40.815
the biggest growth of face recognition technology is in police surveillance,

00:36:41.255 --> 00:36:44.976
where it's used to spot wanted criminals or terror suspects.

00:36:46.096 --> 00:36:46.717
In England,

00:36:47.157 --> 00:36:53.058
super recognizers now work for many police departments in London and elsewhere in the country.

00:36:54.099 --> 00:36:55.960
Detective Kenny Long retired.

00:36:56.279 --> 00:36:59.642
To work with a surveillance company called Digital Barriers.

00:36:59.962 --> 00:37:05.246
He oversees computers that are now almost as good as the best super recognisers,

00:37:05.567 --> 00:37:06.868
says company CEO,

00:37:07.408 --> 00:37:08.209
Zach Doffman.

00:37:08.769 --> 00:37:12.292
The easiest way for people to think about it is it's mimicking the human brain.

00:37:12.592 --> 00:37:13.113
So in essence,

00:37:13.114 --> 00:37:16.696
we're teaching a computer how to replicate what a human being would do,

00:37:17.016 --> 00:37:20.579
but to do so on a fast and unimaginably large scale.

00:37:20.599 --> 00:37:23.341
And that is the real game changer for the technology.

00:37:23.721 --> 00:37:25.663
We can't tell you exactly why,

00:37:26.135 --> 00:37:28.557
The computer sometimes makes the inferences,

00:37:28.617 --> 00:37:29.896
makes the decisions it makes,

00:37:30.257 --> 00:37:32.057
but it just gets better at learning all the time,

00:37:32.378 --> 00:37:35.838
which is exactly how you recognize people that you're familiar with.

00:37:35.878 --> 00:37:40.679
The technology is better able to recognize more people than any human being is able to do.

00:37:41.740 --> 00:37:44.320
But is it as accurate as computer experts say?

00:37:44.900 --> 00:37:52.563
A company called Clearview AI sold facial recognition software to over 600 police forces in the US,

00:37:52.882 --> 00:37:55.523
Europe and elsewhere that contained over three...

00:37:55.824 --> 00:37:56.784
billion faces.

00:37:58.465 --> 00:38:02.146
It was all scraped secretly off sites like Facebook and Twitter.

00:38:03.047 --> 00:38:07.489
This could identify activists at a protest or strangers on the subway,

00:38:08.129 --> 00:38:10.130
revealing not just their names,

00:38:10.230 --> 00:38:12.691
but their address and whom they know.

00:38:15.312 --> 00:38:18.233
Yet despite its supposedly all-seeing eye,

00:38:18.854 --> 00:38:23.434
studies show face recognition technology makes terrible mistakes,

00:38:23.796 --> 00:38:24.655
especially with

00:38:24.775 --> 00:38:25.556
people of colour.

00:38:26.277 --> 00:38:31.940
It's a technology that perpetuates discrimination and harms people of colour and harms black communities specifically.

00:38:32.980 --> 00:38:37.163
Ottawa-based Deborah Raji works for the Algorithmic Justice League,

00:38:37.682 --> 00:38:42.266
a research group fighting hidden racism in face recognition machines.

00:38:42.625 --> 00:38:49.469
She helped conduct an audit of companies like Amazon and found error rates of 35%

00:38:49.889 --> 00:38:52.330
for women of colour compared to 3%

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:53.511
for Caucasians.

00:38:54.264 --> 00:39:00.326
There's already been multiple cases of people of color being misidentified and then falsely arrested.

00:39:00.767 --> 00:39:01.367
The problem?

00:39:02.047 --> 00:39:08.710
These supposedly super recognizer computers sold to police were trained on millions of faces.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:11.331
But most were white or Asian,

00:39:11.631 --> 00:39:13.932
so they make mistakes with people of color.

00:39:14.711 --> 00:39:17.774
But the people that build this technology are just not being careful.

00:39:17.914 --> 00:39:18.054
And,

00:39:18.114 --> 00:39:18.354
you know,

00:39:18.754 --> 00:39:21.115
some could argue maybe they're not being careful because they don't care.

00:39:21.535 --> 00:39:21.715
And,

00:39:21.995 --> 00:39:22.235
you know,

00:39:22.255 --> 00:39:22.836
to be frank,

00:39:22.896 --> 00:39:23.656
I think we should have...

00:39:23.835 --> 00:39:28.978
very strict regulations that force them to behave well whether or not they care.

00:39:30.598 --> 00:39:34.399
Amazon was initially reluctant to reinvent their algorithm,

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:36.280
but in 2021,

00:39:36.800 --> 00:39:41.782
they voluntarily suspended selling face recognition software to police.

00:39:42.583 --> 00:39:45.424
So did the leading maker of police body cameras,

00:39:45.664 --> 00:39:46.164
Axon,

00:39:46.724 --> 00:39:53.307
saying the technology is not currently reliable enough to ethically justify its use.

00:39:54.016 --> 00:39:55.000
I don't think that's enough.

00:39:55.019 --> 00:39:58.635
I don't think we should depend on these companies to make that decision on their own.

00:39:58.675 --> 00:40:00.000
I think there should be some regulatory.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.724
We're a push to pull these products off the market as we continue to have the conversation.

00:40:05.664 --> 00:40:09.307
Computers may eventually overcome this programming bias,

00:40:09.867 --> 00:40:10.768
but ironically,

00:40:11.208 --> 00:40:14.192
it duplicates a problem most humans have too.

00:40:14.672 --> 00:40:17.815
We're very poor at identifying other races.

00:40:18.554 --> 00:40:18.795
Actually,

00:40:18.815 --> 00:40:19.496
when we are born,

00:40:20.257 --> 00:40:26.681
we have no difficulty recognizing faces of people from all races of the world.

00:40:26.981 --> 00:40:28.563
So by about nine months of age,

00:40:28.923 --> 00:40:29.043
the...

00:40:29.304 --> 00:40:32.927
they are only going to be able to recognize the faces of their own race.

00:40:33.207 --> 00:40:36.530
They lose that ability they already had at three months of age.

00:40:36.891 --> 00:40:44.356
People all over the world have the very similar feeling when they encounter people from other nationalities or races.

00:40:45.038 --> 00:40:45.498
That is,

00:40:46.099 --> 00:40:47.680
they think they all look the same.

00:40:47.800 --> 00:40:48.341
In Canada,

00:40:48.461 --> 00:40:49.021
for example,

00:40:49.302 --> 00:40:51.844
we consider this to be a racist notion.

00:40:52.403 --> 00:40:56.528
But this perception actually is universal all over the world.

00:40:57.311 --> 00:40:59.833
When Li first moved to the West from China,

00:41:00.315 --> 00:41:04.338
he says he couldn't tell most Caucasian people apart for two years,

00:41:04.898 --> 00:41:07.039
even his own colleagues and students.

00:41:07.701 --> 00:41:08.922
When I first arrived,

00:41:09.101 --> 00:41:11.864
all the Caucasian faces looked the same to me,

00:41:12.304 --> 00:41:13.525
except they may be,

00:41:13.766 --> 00:41:14.005
you know,

00:41:14.025 --> 00:41:14.547
the only way

00:41:15.748 --> 00:41:16.829
I could tell them apart is

00:41:17.208 --> 00:41:17.449
I say,

00:41:17.509 --> 00:41:17.688
okay,

00:41:17.750 --> 00:41:18.449
this is a man,

00:41:18.630 --> 00:41:19.311
this is a woman,

00:41:19.751 --> 00:41:20.692
this is the old man,

00:41:20.791 --> 00:41:22.434
this is the old woman,

00:41:22.434 --> 00:41:23.114
and a young man,

00:41:23.213 --> 00:41:23.675
young woman.

00:41:23.815 --> 00:41:25.056
These are the things I could do.

00:41:25.400 --> 00:41:27.240
But in terms of who these people are,

00:41:27.601 --> 00:41:28.961
I had tremendous difficulty.

00:41:29.342 --> 00:41:30.402
And slowly,

00:41:30.461 --> 00:41:30.782
slowly,

00:41:31.182 --> 00:41:32.583
my ability to recognize

00:41:33.202 --> 00:41:36.325
Caucasian faces or black faces become better and better.

00:41:37.745 --> 00:41:44.387
So we don't feel badly when we listen to people speaking another language on the bus and can't really understand it.

00:41:45.028 --> 00:41:51.131
But we do feel badly when we struggle to recognize people of an ethnicity other than our own.

00:41:52.907 --> 00:41:57.208
People need to recognize that this is part of human development.

00:41:57.248 --> 00:41:59.009
It's a universal phenomenon.

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:01.150
When I spent a month in China,

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:05.351
I had a very hard time learning the faces of the students in that lab.

00:42:05.751 --> 00:42:08.172
So it's universal and it starts in infancy.

00:42:08.713 --> 00:42:13.313
But the problem is easy to overcome when you're young because it's not about race,

00:42:13.653 --> 00:42:14.813
it's about exposure.

00:42:15.534 --> 00:42:16.315
In one study,

00:42:16.675 --> 00:42:20.016
Korean orphans brought up by Caucasian families in France

00:42:20.511 --> 00:42:24.695
were tested on identifying Korean faces and Caucasian ones.

00:42:25.275 --> 00:42:25.715
Guess what?

00:42:25.976 --> 00:42:28.038
They were better at the white face than the Korean faces.

00:42:28.117 --> 00:42:29.739
It's not to do with your genetics.

00:42:30.099 --> 00:42:31.901
It's to do with your environment.

00:42:32.121 --> 00:42:33.942
And they were brought up in a white environment.

00:42:34.143 --> 00:42:36.344
They got good at recognizing white faces.

00:42:39.127 --> 00:42:44.051
The lesson here is to get your kids to experience lots of different ethnicities when they're younger.

00:42:50.923 --> 00:42:53.365
There are further issues too for everyone,

00:42:53.786 --> 00:42:55.347
say human rights advocates.

00:42:56.027 --> 00:43:01.130
Capturing people's faces as they go about their daily lives is a threat to privacy,

00:43:01.751 --> 00:43:04.652
especially when done without their knowledge.

00:43:05.713 --> 00:43:11.617
We already know that the technology is incredibly dangerous for people of colour when it does not work.

00:43:12.257 --> 00:43:13.077
That being said,

00:43:13.398 --> 00:43:17.059
it's also incredibly dangerous for everyone when it does work.

00:43:18.867 --> 00:43:24.970
It's very difficult to build a facial recognition model without collecting millions of faces.

00:43:25.329 --> 00:43:31.351
And the act of collecting millions of faces is something that will require the violation of privacy.

00:43:32.572 --> 00:43:34.092
So I think my face belongs to me.

00:43:34.813 --> 00:43:38.554
I definitely should have the right to opt out of any system,

00:43:39.374 --> 00:43:44.916
particularly a system that puts me at such risk.

00:43:45.239 --> 00:43:52.446
The question that we as citizens and our politicians and lawmakers need to debate is what is the appropriate use of the technology.

00:43:52.886 --> 00:43:58.031
And whilst I can absolutely see the case to using facial recognition to pull bad guys off the street,

00:43:58.251 --> 00:43:59.532
really bad guys off the street,

00:43:59.833 --> 00:44:06.298
but do I want my kids to be subjected to facial or this kind of AI technology to make sure they're listening to their teachers in class?

00:44:06.519 --> 00:44:07.540
I absolutely do not.

00:44:09.862 --> 00:44:10.884
Yet one country

00:44:11.380 --> 00:44:18.722
is already determined to own the faces of all its citizens in a cross between science and science fiction.

00:44:19.883 --> 00:44:20.583
It's China,

00:44:21.283 --> 00:44:23.684
brave new world of face recognition.

00:44:32.066 --> 00:44:33.167
Here in Shanghai,

00:44:33.767 --> 00:44:37.807
using your face is routine when paying for things like fast food.

00:44:38.583 --> 00:44:41.806
But despite the errors of face recognition technology,

00:44:42.467 --> 00:44:48.030
China's government uses it to monitor many of its 1.4 billion people.

00:44:48.751 --> 00:44:54.056
There are over 300 million CCTV cameras on the streets,

00:44:54.596 --> 00:44:57.177
one camera for every five people.

00:44:57.377 --> 00:44:59.980
The Chinese police surveyed

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:06.425
The jailing system is built towards abusing human rights to implement an Orwellian society.

00:45:07.186 --> 00:45:11.088
Face recognition is used to identify those breaking the law,

00:45:11.389 --> 00:45:12.990
even if they're just jaywalking.

00:45:13.490 --> 00:45:20.117
Suspects are fined and sometimes their photos are displayed on digital billboards for public shaming.

00:45:20.617 --> 00:45:23.159
And I was in Shanghai and I was with a person.

00:45:23.519 --> 00:45:26.282
She showed me like a traffic light.

00:45:26.563 --> 00:45:26.922
She said,

00:45:27.262 --> 00:45:27.402
look,

00:45:27.483 --> 00:45:28.083
this camera.

00:45:29.096 --> 00:45:31.537
If you cross with a pedestrian red light,

00:45:31.657 --> 00:45:34.117
there will be some AI identifying your face,

00:45:34.217 --> 00:45:36.077
and then you will get a ticket sent to you.

00:45:36.798 --> 00:45:37.498
And I was like,

00:45:38.239 --> 00:45:38.858
that's not good.

00:45:38.878 --> 00:45:39.139
I mean,

00:45:39.179 --> 00:45:40.019
that's going too far.

00:45:40.619 --> 00:45:41.099
But yeah,

00:45:41.119 --> 00:45:41.399
I mean,

00:45:41.459 --> 00:45:42.200
that's the future.

00:45:42.240 --> 00:45:42.880
That may happen.

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:48.541
Critics fear a Big Brother society as in Xinjiang province,

00:45:49.001 --> 00:45:55.083
where surveillance systems constantly watch a million of China's Uyghur Muslim minority.

00:45:55.563 --> 00:45:58.344
The whole region is being blanketed by...

00:45:58.663 --> 00:46:00.444
these surveillance systems,

00:46:01.044 --> 00:46:12.867
you can draw essentially a direct line between these systems and people being held in political education camps for practicing religion or expressing their identity.

00:46:13.367 --> 00:46:19.889
So this one million people has been scientifically targeted for their anti-government thinking.

00:46:20.689 --> 00:46:22.530
No other governments could have done that in the past.

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:23.290
No emperor,

00:46:23.830 --> 00:46:24.471
no dictator.

00:46:24.951 --> 00:46:27.471
And and what does it say about human future going forward?

00:46:33.887 --> 00:46:36.809
Yet we are only at the birth of facial recognition.

00:46:37.510 --> 00:46:41.793
Our faces already reveal a lot more than simply who we are.

00:46:42.233 --> 00:46:43.313
When I see your face,

00:46:43.454 --> 00:46:45.494
I'm reading your mind somehow.

00:46:45.775 --> 00:46:47.076
I don't want to get intrusive,

00:46:47.077 --> 00:46:48.556
I don't want to get into your thoughts,

00:46:49.257 --> 00:46:51.257
but I'm reading a lot from your face,

00:46:51.277 --> 00:46:52.239
from your expression.

00:46:52.398 --> 00:46:52.978
So for example,

00:46:52.980 --> 00:46:54.300
if I would focus on your eyes,

00:46:54.840 --> 00:46:58.362
and you are very sad or you are angry or you are very excited,

00:46:58.481 --> 00:46:59.503
I can read that somehow.

00:47:00.262 --> 00:47:01.384
And that's...

00:47:01.623 --> 00:47:01.864
I mean,

00:47:01.903 --> 00:47:03.585
you don't have it in other parts of the body.

00:47:03.806 --> 00:47:05.206
I think the face,

00:47:05.226 --> 00:47:06.407
in particular the eyes,

00:47:06.967 --> 00:47:09.469
is very important to understand each other's emotions.

00:47:10.451 --> 00:47:15.715
Chinese tech companies are already experimenting with emotional recognition,

00:47:16.576 --> 00:47:24.081
technology that may discern our emotions and sexual preferences based on the set of our eyes or mouth.

00:47:25.123 --> 00:47:30.768
Google and Facebook worked on technology to read the twitch of a lip or an eyebrow.

00:47:31.275 --> 00:47:32.596
To decode our thoughts.

00:47:35.357 --> 00:47:39.298
Even surveillance specialist Zach Doffman thinks that's scary.

00:47:40.000 --> 00:47:43.360
If you put enough stuff through the machine learning algorithms,

00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:45.442
they can learn to recognize when a person is happy,

00:47:45.862 --> 00:47:46.781
when a person is sad,

00:47:46.822 --> 00:47:47.623
when they're engaged,

00:47:47.623 --> 00:47:48.583
when they're concentrating,

00:47:48.583 --> 00:47:49.344
when they're listening.

00:47:49.923 --> 00:47:50.183
For me,

00:47:50.423 --> 00:47:50.864
personally,

00:47:51.304 --> 00:47:52.045
this goes too far.

00:47:52.384 --> 00:47:57.786
People will actually also try to build these models to predict things like criminality from your face,

00:47:58.366 --> 00:47:59.947
predict how hireable you are.

00:48:00.644 --> 00:48:04.586
from your face and that kind of thing is becoming more and more common.

00:48:04.827 --> 00:48:11.791
I think that that 1984 vision is really scary but also something that is kind of already here.

00:48:13.773 --> 00:48:21.177
So is face recognition just a threat to our privacy and freedom that will make everyone on earth knowable?

00:48:21.999 --> 00:48:24.599
Or is it a blessing that can also help many?

00:48:25.300 --> 00:48:27.541
Even let we're so happy.

00:48:27.543 --> 00:48:29.983
I'll do what I want as long

00:48:30.431 --> 00:48:30.751
Today,

00:48:30.891 --> 00:48:34.333
Carla Holmes is meeting some congregants after services,

00:48:34.634 --> 00:48:37.254
but she's hoping to see them in a different way.

00:48:37.795 --> 00:48:45.920
Harvard neurologist Barry Sandrew has created a personalized app that should help her recognize some members for the first time.

00:48:52.663 --> 00:48:53.222
Hi Gladys,

00:48:53.223 --> 00:48:53.902
how are you doing?

00:48:54.364 --> 00:48:55.043
Hi Carla.

00:48:55.324 --> 00:48:55.443
Hey,

00:48:55.524 --> 00:48:55.983
Norm,

00:48:56.084 --> 00:48:57.505
how are you?

00:48:57.506 --> 00:48:57.905
I'm doing well,

00:48:57.985 --> 00:48:58.326
Reverend.

00:48:58.346 --> 00:48:58.766
How are you?

00:48:58.786 --> 00:49:00.067
I'm good,

00:49:00.927 --> 00:49:01.748
I'm good.

00:49:01.749 --> 00:49:01.807
Hey,

00:49:01.809 --> 00:49:02.307
Carlos.

00:49:02.588 --> 00:49:02.728
Hi.

00:49:02.988 --> 00:49:03.428
So what are you,

00:49:03.448 --> 00:49:04.929
anything planned for this afternoon?

00:49:05.088 --> 00:49:05.789
Cooking for the week.

00:49:06.170 --> 00:49:06.289
Oh,

00:49:06.390 --> 00:49:06.730
cooking.

00:49:07.050 --> 00:49:07.171
Yeah.

00:49:07.391 --> 00:49:07.911
Okay.

00:49:07.912 --> 00:49:08.932
If you want to make extra,

00:49:09.012 --> 00:49:10.192
I'll put some in my freezer.

00:49:10.251 --> 00:49:10.472
Okay.

00:49:13.673 --> 00:49:14.655
Today was different.

00:49:14.715 --> 00:49:17.776
She recognized me as I came out right away.

00:49:17.936 --> 00:49:19.817
So I felt very good about that,

00:49:20.258 --> 00:49:20.918
you know.

00:49:21.657 --> 00:49:24.300
It's good for her and it's good for us as a congregation.

00:49:24.603 --> 00:49:27.184
Sometimes she passes you by and she doesn't recognize you.

00:49:28.646 --> 00:49:28.846
And,

00:49:29.065 --> 00:49:29.326
I mean,

00:49:29.346 --> 00:49:30.246
it's not a great feeling,

00:49:30.266 --> 00:49:32.186
but I feel like this app could really help that.

00:49:32.867 --> 00:49:39.851
The best part was feeling sure that it wasn't my imagination or it wasn't my best guess,

00:49:40.271 --> 00:49:42.331
that I was sure that that was that person.

00:49:42.871 --> 00:49:44.333
That happens to me almost never.

00:49:44.853 --> 00:49:46.733
And that feeling was wonderful.

00:49:47.373 --> 00:49:48.994
The worst part of it was

00:49:49.335 --> 00:49:52.396
I have to actually look at the app and then look up.

00:49:52.827 --> 00:49:55.170
If I had glasses with this kind of app,

00:49:55.450 --> 00:49:56.911
that would be so much more helpful.

00:49:56.990 --> 00:49:58.172
I wouldn't have to do this.

00:49:58.711 --> 00:49:59.733
I might have to do that,

00:49:59.793 --> 00:50:00.614
but that's okay.

00:50:00.634 --> 00:50:01.974
That's kind of cute to do that,

00:50:02.015 --> 00:50:03.276
so not a big problem.

00:50:07.298 --> 00:50:07.999
Who knows?

00:50:08.759 --> 00:50:15.604
Perhaps someday Carla and others can experience something closer to what most of us take for granted,

00:50:16.305 --> 00:50:20.488
a remarkable superpower for recognizing each other.

